If a test passes locally and fails in CI. Traces captures everything - screenshots at every steps, Network status, Console Log.

1. What traces captures -

a. Screenshots at each and every steps
b. DOM snapshots (complete HTML at each line)
c. Network Status (all HTTP requests/responses)
d. Console Logs
e. Actions timeline (fills, click, assertions in sequence)

2. Enabling traces -

In the playwright.config.ts file, inside the "use" field mention:

use {
traces: "on-first-retry",
},

"traces" keyword expects values that are:

a. "off" - don't capture the traces
b. "on" - capture traces for each and every test
c. "on-first-retry" - capture traces only on the first retry after a failure
d. "retain-on-failure" - capture traces for all tests but deletes and keeps only the one where the test failed

3. Where traces are saved -

After running each test, traces are saved in:

test-results/tests-day32-practice-spec-first-test-chromium.zip

The traces are save in a zip format which can be opened with Test Viewer

4. Viewing the traces:

Method 1: From the command line -

npx playwright show-trace test-results/tests-day32-practice-spec-first-test-chromium.zip

Method 2: From the HTML report -

npx playwright show-report

5. Inside Trace Viewer:

The trace viewer interface contains -

a. Timeline - every action in sequential order (clicks, fills, assertions)
b. Main View - Screenshot or DOM Snapshot at the line of code
c. Inspector on the right - shows page HTML structure at that certain line of code
d. Network Tab - all HTTP requests/response that happened

6. Debugging workflow with traces

Test fails in CI. You pull down the trace:

a. Open it with show-trace
b. Scroll through the timeline to find where things went wrong
c. Click just before the failure — see what the page looked like
d. Click on the failure step — see what the assertion expected vs. what actually happened
e. Check the Network tab — did a request fail? Did a response come back wrong?
f. You now have the exact information to fix the issue (or add more waiting, retry logic, etc.)

7. Traces cleanup:

After few tests the traces folder starts filling up, to delete old traces

rm -rf test-results/ (cleans up the old traces)

npx playwright test (to run new test and generate fresh traces)
